// THE SEAM. This file is the one place where "what shape are the clouds" is answered, and everything
// downstream of it — the march, the lighting, the temporal accumulation, the composite — is written
// against `CloudFieldSample` and never against a producer.
//
// That matters because the programme has two producers by design (Docs/Clouds/ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4):
//
//   P  PROCEDURAL  computed from the component's parameters. Infinite, needs no content, and is the
//                  right tool for the far field, where a cloud's angular size is already below what the
//                  march can resolve and vertical structure is invisible in principle.
//   A  AUTHORED    sampled from voxel volumes an artist sculpted. Arbitrary silhouettes, tunnels, a cloud
//                  shaped like anything at all — the near field and the hero clouds.
//
// BOTH EXIST NOW. A joins at the bottom of this file as a `max` of the two profiles with the winner's
// detail type and density scale — one function, one level up from the `max` the four species already
// take — and NOTHING above this file changed when it landed: the march, the shadow map, the lighting,
// the temporal resolve and the composite are the text they were, because they were written against
// `CloudFieldSample` and never against a producer. The seam was written first in Э0 precisely so that
// the second producer would be a plug rather than a rewrite, and that is what it turned out to be.
//
// THE MEANING OF `Profile` IS LOAD-BEARING and is Nubis's, not a density: it is how deep inside the body
// of the cloud a point is, 0 at the surface and 1 in the core (deck p.85, where it is generated from a
// signed distance field for exactly this gradient). Six different calculations end up hanging off it —
// the silhouette, the frequency blend of the wispy noise, the frequency blend of the billowy noise, the
// multiple-scattering volume, the ambient term, and the early-out — which is why it is worth carrying a
// quantity that means "depth inside" rather than the density a naive implementation would store.
//
// HOW THE DATA ARRIVES. Through two macros, never through a sampler declared here:
//
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE( vec3 texturePosition )   ->  vec4, the four channels of the noise volume, which
//                                                      is the EROSION and nothing else now
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING( vec3 uvw )           ->  vec4, the procedural MODELLING VOLUME — ONE CHANNEL
//                                                      PER SPECIES, in the order of the layer's slots,
//                                                      each of them that species' Dimensional Profile
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED( vec3 uvw )            ->  vec4, four channels of the sculpted-body ATLAS
//
// The third arrives on the same terms as the first two, and the instance list it is addressed with — plus
// the atlas's slab count — comes from Common/CloudAuthored.glslh, which must have been included first.
//
// The march defines them as `texture()` fetches; the unit tests define the first as a call into the same
// CloudNoiseVolumeChannels that baked the volume and the second as a filtered read of the very volume
// Assets::BakeCloudProceduralVolume produced. This is the mechanism SkyScattering.glslh already uses for its
// LUT callbacks, and it exists because a sampler cannot appear in text that is also compiled as C++ —
// which is the only way the maths below gets a test at all. When the macros are undefined the structural
// half of this header still compiles, which is what lets a consumer include it for the types alone.
//
// Dialect rules as in Common/CloudNoise.glslh.

#ifndef CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH

// Requires Common/CloudNoise.glslh, Common/CloudGeometry.glslh and Common/CloudAuthored.glslh to have
// been included first. GLSL has no forward declarations, so include order is the caller's responsibility
// and is stated here rather than discovered as a compile error.

// HOW MANY KINDS OF CLOUD ONE SKY HOLDS AT ONCE. Four, and the number is the width of a texel rather than
// a preference: a species owns one CHANNEL of the profile table, which is Unreal's arrangement exactly
// (EpicDoc_CloudMaterial.md §2-3 fixes four types to R, G, B and A). Graphic::kCloudSpeciesSlots is the
// same constant on the C++ side. The LIBRARY on disk is not limited by this; a single LAYER is.
#define CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS 4

// What every producer returns and what the whole renderer consumes.
//
// THE LAST THREE ARE THE WINNING SPECIES', and they travel here rather than in the parameter block for a
// reason that only exists once a sky has more than one kind of cloud in it. What a cloud is MADE OF — how
// deeply its edge erodes, how much matter is in it, how opaque that matter is — is a property of the kind
// of cloud, so with four kinds in one sky it stops being a constant of the march and becomes a property of
// the SAMPLE. Carried from the species whose profile won the union, never blended: a blend of a
// stratocumulus and a congestus is a third thing that is neither, and the seam where two bodies meet is
// exactly where a floating average is visible as a smear of the wrong character.
struct CloudFieldSample
{
    float Profile;          // 0..1, depth inside the cloud body (see the header note)
    float DetailType;       // 0 = wispy, 1 = billowy — selects which erosion the up-rez applies
    float DensityScale;     // per-sample multiplier applied AFTER erosion (deck p.118)
    float DetailFactor;     // the winner's multiplier on the layer's erosion depth
    float ExtinctionFactor; // the winner's multiplier on the layer's extinction
};

// Everything the procedural producer needs, unpacked from the GPU block by Common/CloudParams.glslh.
// Distances are KILOMETRES, matching Common/CloudGeometry.glslh.
struct CloudFieldParams
{
    float DetailTileKm;     // period of the erosion field
    float DetailStrength;   // how deeply the erosion cuts into the profile, for the LAYER
    float DensityScale;     // the LAYER's density multiplier; the species' own is folded in per sample
    int   SpeciesCount;     // how many channels of the modelling volume are filled, 0..CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS

    // Per species k: x DetailCharacter, y DetailFactor, z DensityFactor, w ExtinctionFactor. Four numbers
    // that describe what that kind of cloud is made of, and all four are read.
    vec4 SpeciesEdge[CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS];

    // WHERE THE MODELLING VOLUME IS, and it is two numbers rather than a matrix because the region is
    // axis-aligned by construction: the minimum corner of the region in world kilometres, and the
    // reciprocal of its side. Both are the CPU's — Assets::CloudProceduralRegionOriginKm decides the
    // corner and snaps it to the lump lattice, and the march only maps a position through it.
    vec2  RegionOriginKm;
    float InvRegionSizeKm;

    vec3 WindOffsetKm; // accumulated wind displacement, subtracted from the sample position
};

// There are deliberately NO SEEDS AND NO OCTAVE COUNTS here. Both are properties of the BAKE — they
// decide what is in the volume, not how it is read — so carrying them into the march would be four
// parameters that every sample copies and no sample uses. They live in the bake pass's push constant,
// which is the only place they are read.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The anisotropy of the EROSION octave. THREE NUMBERS, NOT ONE SCALAR.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// There were two triples here and there is one, because the other described the COVERAGE field's own
// anisotropy — and there is no coverage field any more. Where a cloud is and how tall it is are decided by
// the lumps of the modelling volume now (Engine/Assets/CloudProceduralVolume.hpp), and a lump's stretch is
// three radii of an ellipsoid rather than three frequencies of a noise: exact where this was a ratio, and
// authored per species by the lattice's own anisotropy.
//
// What survives is the triple that never described placement at all. Unreal's graph carries anisotropy on
// all three of its octaves (ShapeModel.md §11.2, coefficients at L1124), and the third one is INVERTED —
// its vertical coefficient is BELOW its horizontal one, so that octave is stretched UPWARD rather than
// flattened. Our erosion octave has no counterpart in the graph, so it takes the remaining one, and the
// fact that the remaining one is the inverted one is a small piece of luck: wisps that are taller than
// they are wide is what a torn cloud edge looks like.
//
// Y IS UP HERE AND Z IS UP IN UNREAL, so the middle number is Unreal's third.
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_X 1.0f
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Y 0.833f // 25 / 30 — BELOW one: this octave is stretched upward
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Z 1.333f // 40 / 30

// THE CEILING ON THE THREE MATERIAL FACTORS, and it is here because a `max(x, 0)` is only half a bound.
//
// Detail Factor, Density Factor and Extinction Factor all reach this file as a multiplier on a LAYER
// slider, and every one of those products is written `slider * factor`. A slider at zero is not an edge
// case — it is the position the artist is invited to use, and the whole of
// CloudFieldDensity.AtZeroStrengthTheProfileSurvivesUntouched exists to assert that turning the erosion
// off works. But `0 * (+inf)` is NaN, not zero, and a NaN density is the one value the march cannot
// survive: it propagates into the optical depth, from there into the transmittance, and shows as a black
// or fully transparent hole in the cloud with nothing in the log.
//
// A lower bound alone does not close it, because `max(+inf, 0)` is `+inf`. So the factors are bounded on
// BOTH sides at the point they enter a sample.
//
// WHY EIGHT AND NOT FOUR. Eight is the ceiling Assets::ValidateCloudTypeShape already enforces on the
// procedural side (Engine/Assets/CloudTypeData.cpp), so on that path this bound is the identity and can
// change no shipped pixel. It is four times the reflected Range of the authored side's own three factors
// (ECS::HeroCloudData) and four times the layer's Density Scale slider (Range(0, 2)), so it is the
// identity on every value the editor can produce as well.
//
// It is restated in the shader rather than trusted to those validators because only ONE of them exists.
// The reflected Range constrains a slider and nothing else: a scene file carrying a value outside it is
// deserialised verbatim (Core/Serialize/ComponentRegistry.cpp clamps nothing), survives the packer's
// `std::max(x, 0.0f)` unchanged, and arrives here. A shader must not depend on a CPU-side check it cannot
// see, and on the authored path there is no check to depend on.
#define CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR_MAX 8.0f

// One bound, applied wherever a factor is read out of a species row, an instance row or the layer block
// on its way into a product with a slider.
#define CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( x ) min( max( x, 0.0f ), CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR_MAX )

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE MODELLING VOLUME: what replaced the coverage threshold, and why it had to
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHAT STOOD HERE. A weighted blend of the noise volume's two Alligator channels, mapped through its own
// cumulative distribution so that `Coverage` addressed a fraction of sky, thresholded by a smoothstep, and
// multiplied by a two-dimensional table `f(height in the envelope, how deep inside the patch)`. Every one
// of those pieces was individually right, and the construction had a defect NONE of them could fix:
//
//     Alligator = best - second, and that field is ZERO wherever two feature points contribute equally.
//
// There is a wall of zeros between every pair of cells. Two lobes therefore cannot merge at any setting of
// any slider, and the sky comes out as a deck of separate cushions rather than as a convective mass. Three
// tasks measured it independently — T2 on the field, T0 on the profile, T3 on four independent placement
// fields — and all three worked around it.
//
// WHAT IS HERE INSTEAD is variant C of Docs/Clouds/ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §3 point 2, approved as decision
// D-1 and then substituted for the cheaper threshold: the profile is generated not as "coverage times a
// gradient" but as a SUM OF SMOOTHED VOLUMETRIC LUMPS JOINED BY AN EXPONENTIAL SMOOTH MINIMUM, and the
// profile IS the normalised distance field of that join. The smooth minimum has no wall of zeros; merging
// is its defining property rather than a setting.
//
// It is not evaluated here. Hundreds of lumps per region against ninety-five million field samples a frame
// is the wrong way round, so the join is baked into a camera-centric 3D texture whenever the parameters
// change or the region shifts — never per frame — and the march performs ONE FETCH. That fetch answers for
// all four species at once, where the arrangement it replaces cost one 2D fetch plus one 3D fetch per live
// species.
//
// THE HEIGHT-DEPENDENT DOMAIN WARP IS STILL NOT HERE, and the measurement that removed it stands: Unreal
// displaces its base octave's coordinates by a second noise on a height-dependent vector, because its
// placement pattern is a two-dimensional texture and the warp is the only three-dimensionality it has.
// Measured on Clouds_Demo at three elevations by two azimuths, the whole travel of that control moved the
// contrast by at most 0.018 with no consistent sign. Our field is three-dimensional by construction and is
// now MORE so, not less: a lump has three radii and an altitude.

// The procedural modelling volume's HEIGHT, which the march needs as a compile-time number to pull the
// vertical fetch in by half a texel. Assets::kCloudProceduralVolumeHeight is the same number on the C++
// side, and Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudField asserts the two statements agree — that suite compiles this
// header as C++, which is the only place the comparison can be made at all.
//
// ONE EXTENT AND NOT THREE. The first draft declared the width and the depth beside it "for symmetry",
// and neither was ever read: the horizontal mapping is `(world - origin) * invRegionSize`, which is in
// texture units already and never needs to know how many texels there are. A sabotage run caught it —
// setting the height to 64 left both suites GREEN, and looking at why turned up two dead macros as well
// as the missing assertion. A constant nobody reads is a setting nobody reads with extra steps
// (contract §1.3).
//
// PROCEDURAL AND NOT MODELLING IN THE NAME, because Common/CloudAuthored.glslh already spends
// CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_* on the SCULPTED body's 128 x 64 x 128. Two volumes, two producers, two sets of
// extents — and the first draft of this file called both of them the same thing, which the shader compiler
// caught as a macro redefinition rather than as a silently wrong fetch.
#define CLOUD_PROCEDURAL_VOLUME_HEIGHT 32.0f

// WHERE A WORLD POSITION LANDS IN THE VOLUME.
//
// HORIZONTALLY IT IS NOT CLAMPED AND MUST NOT BE. The bake splats every lump at its wrapped positions too,
// so the volume is exactly periodic — and REPEAT sampling of a periodic volume is the DEGENERATE FAR PATH
// of §3 point 3: past the region the sky is the region again, at a distance where a cloud's angular size is
// already below what the march resolves (§4.5). Clamping instead would put a smear of the edge column
// across everything beyond 24 km.
//
// VERTICALLY IT IS CLAMPED TO THE TEXEL CENTRES, and that is not symmetry-breaking for its own sake: the
// volume spans the shell exactly, every sampler in this engine is REPEAT, and without the clamp a height
// fraction of 1 wraps to the bottom row — so the top of the layer would grow the bases of its own clouds
// back on top of itself. The same clamp, for the same reason, that the profile table needed.
vec3 CloudProceduralVolumeUvw( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 windPos )
{
    float halfV = 0.5f / CLOUD_PROCEDURAL_VOLUME_HEIGHT;

    return vec3( ( windPos.x - params.RegionOriginKm.x ) * params.InvRegionSizeKm,
                 clamp( heightFraction, halfV, 1.0f - halfV ),
                 ( windPos.z - params.RegionOriginKm.y ) * params.InvRegionSizeKm );
}

#if defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE ) && defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING ) && defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED )

// The procedural producer — ONE FETCH, four species.
//
// WHAT IT USED TO BE: a loop over the live species, each of them costing a 3D fetch of the noise volume at
// its own scale, a quantile map, a smoothstep threshold and a second fetch of the profile table, guarded by
// a cheap rejection that was itself a fetch. What it is now is a single trilinear read of the modelling
// volume, whose channel k is species k's Dimensional Profile — already three-dimensional, already fused,
// already normalised, because the bake did all of it (Engine/Assets/CloudProceduralVolume.hpp).
//
// WHY THE SPECIES ARE STILL JOINED BY `max` AND NOT BY THE SMOOTH MINIMUM. Inside a species the lumps fuse,
// because a convective mass is what a species IS; across species they must not. Decision D-14's target
// frame is a low stratocumulus deck with a congestus tower standing in it and partly behind it — two
// KINDS of cloud overlapping, each keeping its own character — and a smooth minimum there would grow a
// third body in the gap between them that is neither. `Profile` means "how deep inside a body of cloud this
// point is", so the deeper of two answers is the answer, and the order the species were listed in cannot
// reach the result.
//
// THE WINNER TAKES ALL FOUR MATERIAL NUMBERS and shares none of them. See the note on CloudFieldSample: a
// blend of a stratocumulus' and a congestus' detail character is a third character that is neither, and the
// seam where two bodies meet is exactly where a floating average shows as a smear of the wrong kind.
CloudFieldSample CloudSampleProceduralField( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudFieldSample result;
    result.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;

    // THE WIND IS A SHIFT OF THE SAMPLE AND NOT OF THE VOLUME, which is what keeps the wind free: the
    // region's origin is chosen around the camera MINUS this same offset (Assets::
    // CloudProceduralRegionOriginKm, called with the shifted camera), so the point being asked about is
    // inside the region that was baked for it however far the wind has run.
    vec3 windPos = vec3( positionKm.x - params.WindOffsetKm.x, positionKm.y - params.WindOffsetKm.y,
                         positionKm.z - params.WindOffsetKm.z );

    vec4 volume = CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING( CloudProceduralVolumeUvw( params, heightFraction, windPos ) );

    int speciesCount = min( params.SpeciesCount, CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS );

    for ( int slot = 0; slot < speciesCount; ++slot )
    {
        float speciesProfile = clamp( volume[slot], 0.0f, 1.0f );
        if ( speciesProfile <= result.Profile )
            continue;

        vec4 edge = params.SpeciesEdge[slot];

        result.Profile          = speciesProfile;
        result.DetailType       = clamp( edge.x, 0.0f, 1.0f );
        result.DetailFactor     = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( edge.y );
        result.DensityScale     = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( params.DensityScale ) * CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( edge.z );
        result.ExtinctionFactor = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( edge.w );
    }

    return result;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SLOT A — THE AUTHORED PRODUCER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What slot A answers with: a field sample AND how much of the procedural field this point's instances
// are suppressing. Two values because the cutout is not a property of the sample — it decides what the
// OTHER producer is allowed to contribute — and this dialect has no `out` parameters, deliberately.
struct CloudAuthoredResult
{
    CloudFieldSample Field;
    float            Cutout; // 0 = the procedural field stands, 1 = it is entirely suppressed here
};

// Sample every hero cloud whose bounds contain this point, and union them.
//
// THE COST IS THE WHOLE DESIGN OF THIS FUNCTION, because it is called once per march step AND once per
// SHADOW-RAY step, which is about thirty times more often again. Three gates in order of price:
//
//   1. THE COUNT. A scene with no hero clouds does not enter the loop at all. That is what makes the
//      producer cost zero when it is absent rather than almost zero (ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4.4). The
//      frames measure it: five of the protocol's six points render BYTE FOR BYTE the file they rendered
//      before this producer existed, and the sixth moves 199 pixels of 980 480 by one 8-bit level —
//      which is not this loop but the recompiled SPIR-V scheduling the PROCEDURAL producer's floating
//      point differently, because the file it lives in changed. Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md §A0.
//   2. THE AXIS-ALIGNED BOX, six compares. It is a superset of the instance's real bounds and it is here
//      because it is four times cheaper than the map that follows.
//   3. THE EXACT BOX, after the map. A rotated instance's AABB contains up to 3.4x its volume, and a
//      point in that difference must not fetch: every sampler in this engine is REPEAT, so a coordinate
//      outside [0, 1] would fetch the far side of the body and grow a mirror of its own flank beside it.
//
// The union rule is the SPECIES' RULE, one level up (§4.3): profile means "how deep inside a body", so
// the deeper answer is the answer, and the winner's material numbers travel with it rather than being
// blended — a blend of two bodies' detail character is a third character that is neither, and the seam
// between them is exactly where a floating average shows as a smear.
CloudAuthoredResult CloudSampleAuthoredField( CloudFieldParams params, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudAuthoredResult result;
    result.Field.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.Field.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;
    result.Cutout                 = 0.0f;

    int count = CLOUD_AUTHORED_COUNT;

    // THE ABSENT CASE COSTS WHAT IT COST AT A0 AND NOT ONE INSTRUCTION MORE: one integer load and one
    // compare, per field sample, of which a frame takes on the order of 95 million. The slab count is
    // read AFTER this line and not beside the count above, so a sky with no hero clouds in it does not
    // load it — which is the difference between "zero when absent" holding and drifting by a load per
    // sample every time this producer gains a global (ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4.4).
    if ( count == 0 )
        return result;

    int slabCount = CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLAB_COUNT;

    for ( int i = 0; i < count; ++i )
    {
        CloudAuthoredInstance instance = CLOUD_AUTHORED_INSTANCE( i );

        if ( !CloudAuthoredInBounds( instance, positionKm ) )
            continue;

        vec3 uvw = CloudAuthoredLocalUvw( instance, positionKm );
        if ( !CloudAuthoredInVolume( uvw ) )
            continue;

        // THE FETCH IS INTO THE ATLAS, and which body it lands in is the instance's own slab origin. That
        // is the whole of what phase A2 added to this loop: one multiply-add on the depth coordinate, so
        // eight DIFFERENT sculpted bodies cost the march exactly what one did.
        vec4 voxel =
             CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED( CloudAuthoredAtlasUvw( uvw, instance.BoundsMax.w, slabCount ) );

        float strength = CloudAuthoredStrength( instance );

        // THE CUTOUT UNIONS ACROSS INSTANCES AND IS NOT TIED TO THE WINNER. Two hero clouds standing in
        // each other's boxes each suppress the procedural field over their own body, and the procedural
        // field has to be gone from BOTH — taking only the winner's would let a blob grow through the
        // one that lost by a hair.
        //
        // It is the volume's ENVELOPE channel and not the box, which is the difference between a hero
        // cloud that pushes the deck away from its own skin and one that cuts a rectangular hole in the
        // sky around itself. The envelope is the body dilated by the bake's margin, so it is above zero
        // wherever the profile is and extends past it: a tunnel through the body narrower than that
        // margin is inside the envelope and shows sky, which is what a tunnel is for.
        result.Cutout = max( result.Cutout, voxel.w * CloudAuthoredCutout( instance ) );

        float profile = voxel.x * strength;
        if ( profile <= result.Field.Profile )
            continue;

        result.Field.Profile      = profile;
        result.Field.DetailType   = clamp( voxel.y, 0.0f, 1.0f );
        result.Field.DetailFactor = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( instance.Row0.w );
        // The layer's density, this body's factor and the volume's own per-voxel scale, in that order —
        // the same three-level composition the procedural producer performs, so a Density Scale of 1
        // means the same thing on both sides of the seam.
        result.Field.DensityScale =
             CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( params.DensityScale ) * CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( instance.Row1.w ) * voxel.z;
        result.Field.ExtinctionFactor = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( instance.Row2.w );
    }

    return result;
}

// THE SEAM ITSELF. One producer that unions up to four species, one that unions up to eight sculpted
// bodies, and the max of the two — the same `max`, one level up, which is what both unions below it were
// written to make obvious.
//
// THE AUTHORED SIDE GOES FIRST, and not because either order changes the union: `max` is commutative and
// Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudAuthored asserts it on the whole sample. It goes first because it decides
// whether the procedural side is computed AT ALL. Inside a hero cloud the cutout is 1, the procedural
// field is entirely suppressed there, and computing it would be a 3D fetch, a profile fetch and a
// quantile map thrown away — so the branch below is a saving in exactly the place a hero cloud makes the
// frame most expensive.
CloudFieldSample SampleCloudField( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudAuthoredResult authored = CloudSampleAuthoredField( params, positionKm );

    CloudFieldSample result;
    result.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;

    if ( authored.Cutout < 1.0f )
    {
        result = CloudSampleProceduralField( params, heightFraction, positionKm );

        // THE CUTOUT SCALES THE PROFILE RATHER THAN THRESHOLDING IT, so the procedural field fades out
        // over the envelope's own margin instead of ending at a surface. A hard test would put a visible
        // edge in the deck at the exact distance the envelope reaches, and that edge would be a shape
        // nobody authored — the bake's margin — drawn in somebody else's cloud.
        result.Profile = result.Profile * ( 1.0f - authored.Cutout );
    }

    if ( authored.Field.Profile > result.Profile )
        result = authored.Field;

    return result;
}

// The up-rez: turn the low-frequency profile into something with a cloud's edge.
//
// The erosion is a REMAP, not a subtraction (deck p.120, `ValueErosion`). The difference shows at the
// core: subtracting noise thins a dense cloud as much as a wispy one, so a cumulus turns translucent in
// the middle, while remapping leaves anything already deep inside the body at full density and only cuts
// where the profile was already falling off.
//
// `DetailType` selects between the volume's two CHARACTERS — wispy and billowy — and each of them is
// itself a frequency blend driven by the profile. That is the deck's composite on p.122 in full rather
// than the two-channel half of it that stood here, and the difference is that the erosion's frequency now
// follows how deep inside the body the sample is instead of being constant through the whole cloud.
float CloudSampleDensity( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm )
{
    if ( field.Profile <= 0.0f )
        return 0.0f;

    vec3 windPos = vec3( positionKm.x - params.WindOffsetKm.x, positionKm.y - params.WindOffsetKm.y,
                         positionKm.z - params.WindOffsetKm.z );

    // ANISOTROPIC, and it was not. The erosion was the one octave here still sampled as a cube, which
    // gives a torn cloud edge the proportions of gravel; the graph has anisotropy on all three of its
    // octaves. This one takes the INVERTED triple — vertical period longer than horizontal — so the wisps
    // it cuts are taller than they are wide, which is what a shred of cloud being carried upward looks
    // like.
    float tile      = max( params.DetailTileKm, 1e-4f );
    vec3  detailPos = vec3( windPos.x * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_X / tile, windPos.y * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Y / tile,
                            windPos.z * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Z / tile );

    vec4 noise = CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE( detailPos );

    // THE DECK'S OWN COMPOSITE (p.122), and it uses all four channels rather than two of them.
    //
    // Each character of erosion is a blend of ITS OWN pair across frequency, and the blend is driven by the
    // profile — deep inside the body the coarse octave, out at the edge the fine one, which is the
    // frequency-follows-depth relation the deck states on p.101 and p.105. The wispy end blends linearly;
    // the billowy end blends on the fourth root, so a billowy cloud reaches its fine octave much earlier
    // and its edge breaks into lobes rather than fading into one smooth frequency.
    //
    // Only THEN do the two characters mix, on DetailType. Written the other way round — pick a channel by
    // type and never blend by depth — the erosion has one frequency everywhere inside a cloud, which is
    // what the two-channel version this replaces did.
    float wispy   = mix( noise.x, noise.y, field.Profile );
    float billowy = mix( noise.z, noise.w, pow( field.Profile, 0.25f ) );

    float detailField = clamp( mix( wispy, billowy, clamp( field.DetailType, 0.0f, 1.0f ) ), 0.0f, 1.0f );

    // THE EROSION IS WEIGHTED BY DEPTH, and this is the difference between clouds and fog.
    //
    // Applied uniformly, the erosion subtracts as much from the core of a cloud as from its edge — and
    // since the profile is only high in a thin band anyway, a strength of 0.5 removes most of the layer
    // and what survives is a translucent veil. That is not a tuning failure: a cut of fixed depth into a
    // field whose typical value is small removes the field.
    //
    // Weighting by (1 - Profile) puts the whole cut where the cloud is already ending. The core, which is
    // deep inside the body, keeps its density and stays solid; the edge, which is barely inside it, is
    // eaten into billows and wisps. It is the same principle the deck states as a frequency blend driven
    // by the dimensional profile (p.101, p.105) — the profile decides how much detail belongs here — and
    // it is why that field means "depth inside the body" rather than "density".
    // THE DEPTH OF THE CUT IS THE WINNING SPECIES', multiplied into the layer's own strength here rather
    // than folded together on the CPU as it was while a layer had one kind of cloud in it. A lenticular
    // has to stay a smooth lens and a cirrus has to stay shredded when the two are in the same sky, and a
    // single product formed once could only describe one of them.
    // THE FACTOR IS BOUNDED ON BOTH SIDES AND NOT ONLY BELOW. `max(x, 0)` leaves `+inf` intact, and this
    // product's other operand is a slider whose zero position is the whole subject of the test below it —
    // so the unbounded version turns "erosion off" into a NaN density for every sample in the body. See
    // the note on CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR_MAX: on every value either producer can legally carry, this bound
    // is the identity.
    float edgeWeight = 1.0f - clamp( field.Profile, 0.0f, 1.0f );
    float erosion    = detailField *
                    clamp( params.DetailStrength * CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( field.DetailFactor ), 0.0f, 1.0f ) *
                    edgeWeight;

    float density = CloudRemap( field.Profile, erosion, 1.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f );

    return clamp( density * field.DensityScale, 0.0f, 1.0f );
}

#endif // CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE && CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING && CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED

#endif // CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH
